{"type":"message","id":"8afa3357","parentId":"8226d310","timestamp":"2026-05-28T14:25:02.810Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The cron/HEARTBEAT.md reminders are triggered by OpenClaw's built-in heartbeat system, which reads HEARTBEAT.md and sends reminders based on the cron event provider. The cron configuration is not in openclaw.json or config.yaml.\n\nThe issue is that all reminders are being triggered at around 22:23-22:24 CST, which is 14:23-14:24 UTC. And the HEARTBEAT.md tasks are at specific times (6:00, 7:00, 11:00, 13:00, 17:00, 18:30, 23:50).\n\nActually, looking at the inbound metadata, it says `\"provider\": \"cron-event\"` and the current time is 2026-05-28 22:23 CST (14:23 UTC). The reminders are coming in rapid succession because all the HEARTBEAT.md tasks are being checked at once.\n\nWait - the heartbeat system doesn't schedule individual times. The HEARTBEAT.md just lists tasks with time slots. The heartbeat poll fires, reads HEARTBEAT.md, and then the LLM determines if any task needs attention based on the current time.\n\nThe problem is that all these reminders were triggered in rapid succession at 22:23-22:24. This suggests the heartbeat system is configured to fire frequently (maybe every minute) and the LLM is being asked to check all tasks each time.\n\nBut actually, the reminder content says things like \"Read HEARTBEAT.md, check the 11:00-12:30 吃午餐 task\" - this means the cron system is generating specific per-task reminders. This looks like a scheduled reminder system that was configured to fire at specific times.\n\nThe current time is 10:23 PM local time. If the cron used UTC instead of local time:\n- 6:00 CST = 22:00 previous day UTC → fires at 6:00 CST? No...\n\nWait, the heartbeat poll messages include \"Current time: Thursday, May 28th, 2026 — 10:23 PM (Asia/Shanghai)\". So the timezone is correctly set to Asia/Shanghai.\n\nThe issue is more likely that all the HEARTBEAT.md tasks are being checked simultaneously in a single heartbeat poll and the LLM is triggering all of them. Or the cron event provider is firing rapidly for each task defined in HEARTBEAT.md.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The HEARTBEAT.md has been loaded as workspace context and contains the schedule. The heartbeat polling mechanism reads HEARTBEAT.md, sees all the time-based tasks, and the LLM checks if each one needs attention based on the current time. Since it's 10:23 PM, the LLM correctly identifies that only the 23:50 sleep task is relevant... but the cron provider is generating individual reminders for EACH task with specific instructions like \"check the 11:00-12:30 吃午餐 task\".\n\nThis suggests there's a cron scheduler configured somewhere that creates separate scheduled events for each HEARTBEAT.md task. Let me look for where these schedule events are defined.\n\nActually, looking more carefully, these are separate messages from the user/system, each triggered by \"A scheduled reminder has been triggered.\" This is the cron event provider sending reminders. The timestamps show they're all within 1 minute of each other, which means the cron scheduler is firing all of them at once.\n\nThe issue is likely that the cron scheduler has UTC timezone configured instead of Asia/Shanghai, so all tasks scheduled at CST times are being mapped to UTC and firing at wrong times.\n\nLet me just tell the user what I found and suggest checking the OpenClaw cron event configuration. Since I can't find the cron config in the standard config files, it might be in a separate cron config or in the OpenClaw database.\n\nActually, let me check the OpenClaw cron provider configuration more carefully.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_JhKV8ipCV5iwTFdIz8DC7420","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat /root/.openclaw/openclaw.json | python3 -c \"import sys,json; c=json.load(sys.stdin); print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in c.items() if k not in ['meta','wiza