{"type":"message","id":"614f3e93","parentId":"9b5564c9","timestamp":"2026-05-28T14:25:55.453Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the issue. Let me analyze the cron expressions and the `runningAtMs` values.\n\nThe `runningAtMs` for all jobs is `1779978197801`. Let me convert this:\n- 1779978197801 ms = 1779978197.801 seconds\n\nLet me convert this UTC timestamp:\n1779978197 seconds since epoch. Let me think... \n- 2026-05-28 14:23:17 UTC would be around... let me calculate:\n- 2026-01-01 00:00:00 UTC = 1767225600 seconds\n- 2026-05-28 = 148 days into 2026 (Jan 31 + Feb 28 + Mar 31 + Apr 30 + May 27 = 147, so May 28 is day 148)\n- 148 * 86400 = 12787200\n- 1767225600 + 12787200 = 1780012800... hmm, that's slightly more than 1779978197.\n\nWait, let me just use the system to convert. But I can also check: 14:23 UTC is 22:23 CST (UTC+8). So the runningAtMs timestamp represents 14:23 UTC = 22:23 CST.\n\nNow the `nextRunAtMs` values:\n- test_1435: 1779950100000 → check if this is 14:35 UTC or CST\n- wake_up: 1779921000000 → \"30 6 * * *\"\n- breakfast: 1779924600000 → \"30 7 * * *\"\n- lunch: 1779939000000 → \"30 11 * * *\"\n- nap: 1779945000000 → \"10 13 * * *\"\n- dinner: 1779965100000 → \"45 18 * * *\"\n- sleep: 1779897000000 → \"50 23 * * *\"\n- run_reminder: 1779958800000 → \"0 17 * * *\"\n\nThe `runningAtMs` is 1779978197801 for all. This is the timestamp when all jobs were just triggered simultaneously.\n\nNow, looking at the test_1435 job: it has `runningAtMs: 1779978197801` (which is 22:23 CST / 14:23 UTC), but its cron expression is `35 14 * * *`, meaning it should fire at 14:35. The current time is 14:23 UTC, and it's firing at 14:23 instead of 14:35. That's odd.\n\nBut all other jobs also have `runningAtMs: 1779978197801`, including dinner which already has a `lastRunAtMs` of 1779878700048. Let me convert dinner's lastRunAtMs:\n1779878700 seconds. 1767225600 = Jan 1, 2026. 1779878700 - 1767225600 = 12653100 seconds = 146.4 days. That would be around May 27, 2026 18:45 CST, which is correct for the dinner reminder (18:45 CST).\n\nWait, but the runningAtMs should only change when the job is actually running. It seems like someone ran them all at once, or the cron system triggered them all.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully, the issue might not be a timezone problem. The `runningAtMs: 1779978197801` timestamp seems to be when the last cron evaluation happened, and the scheduler decided to fire all jobs. This could be because:\n\n1. The cron daemon/service was restarted at 22:23 CST and decided to catch up on all missed jobs\n2. There's a bug where the scheduler is interpreting times incorrectly\n\nWait, let me recalculate. The `runningAtMs` for ALL jobs is identical: 1779978197801. This strongly suggests the scheduler just ran all of them simultaneously. \n\nThe test_1435 cron is `35 14 * * *` which should fire at 14:35. But all jobs fired at... let me convert 1779978197801:\n\nActually, 1779978197801 ms. Let me be more careful:\n- 1779978197.801 seconds\n- 1767225600 seconds = Jan 1, 2026 00:00:00 UTC\n- Difference = 12752597.801 seconds\n\nWait that's way too many days. Let me use Python to convert.\n\nActually I should just use exec to convert these timestamps. But let me think about what's more important to tell the user.\n\nThe key findings:\n1. The cron jobs use standard cron expressions like `35 14 * * *` (test), `30 6 * * *` (wake), etc.\n2. ALL jobs have the same `runningAtMs: 1779978197801`, meaning they all just fired simultaneously\n3. This suggests either a scheduler bug or the scheduler was restarted and caught up on all jobs\n\nThe most likely explanation: OpenClaw's cron scheduler was restarted (perhaps via `kj` or gateway restart) around 22:23, and it fired all pending jobs. The test job (14:35) and the exercise job (17:00) would have been \"missed\" since last restart, and the scheduler caught up.\n\nBut wait - the dinner job has lastRunAtMs showing it ran successfully earlier today. And the sleep job (23:50) hasn't even had its scheduled time yet but still fired.\n\nActually, looking at the \"wakeMode\": \"now\" setting - this might be the issue. \"wakeMode\": \"now\" might mean \"when the scheduler wakes up/restarts, run this job immediately.\" If the scheduler was restarted, all jobs with wakeMode: \"now\" would fire immediately.\n\nSo the fix would be to change wakeMode from \"now\" to something else, or fix the scheduler's behavior.\n\nLet me tell the user what I found and suggest fixing the wakeMode.\n\nActually wait - I should check the nextRunAtMs values to verify:\n- sleep job: expr \"50 23 * * *\", nextRunAtMs: 1779897000000\n- wake_up job: expr \"30 6 * * *\", nextRunAtMs: 1779921000000\n\nThese nextRunAtMs values seem to represent May 29, 2026 at the correct times. Let me verify:\n- sleep: 50 23 on May 28 → May 28 23:50 CST = May 28 15:50 UTC\n  - 1779897000... Let me think about whether this is CST or UTC.\n\nIf nextRunAtMs for sleep (23:50 CST) is 1779897000000 ms:\n- In UTC, 23:50 CST = 15:50 UTC on May 28\n- May 28 = Jan 1 + 147 days = 1767225600 + 147*86400 = 1767225600 + 12700800 = 1779926400 seconds for May 29 00:00 UTC\n- Actually, Jan 1 is day 0, May 28 is day 147 (Jan 0-Jan31=31, Feb=28, Mar=31, Apr=30, May 27=147, so May 28 = day 148)\n  - 1767225600 + 148*86400 = 1767225600 + 12787200 = 1780012800 for midnight May 29 UTC\n  - Wait, I need to be more careful. Jan 1st is day 0. So May 28 is day 147 (31+28+31+30+27 = 147). So May 28 00:00 UTC = 1767225600 + 147*86400 = 1767225600 + 12700800 = 1779926400.\n\n- sleep nextRunAtMs = 1779897000000 ms = 1779897000 s\n  - 1779897000 - 1779926400 = -29400 seconds = -8.17 hours\n  - So it's 8 hours before May 28 00:00 UTC = May 27 16:00 UTC = May 28 00:00 CST. That doesn't match 23:50.\n\nWait, this isn't right. Let me just use the date command to convert these.\n\nActually, I'm spending too much time on manual calculation. Let me just tell the user what I found and fix the issue. The key insight is:\n\n1. The cron jobs are configured with wakeMode: \"now\"\n2. Probably the gateway/scheduler was restarted, causing all jobs to fire immediately\n3. I should change wakeMode to something more appropriate\n\nLet me just report this to the user and ask if they want me to fix it. Or better yet, just fix it.\n\nActually, I think wakeMode: \"now\" might be intentional for some jobs but not others. Let me look at the OpenClaw docs or just change them all.\n\nWait, I should be more careful here. The \"wakeMode\": \"now\" field - I don't know exactly what it means in OpenClaw's cron system. Let me check the docs.\n\nActually, let me first confirm by converting timestamps, then report to user.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"找到原因了。让我确认下时间戳。"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_aLnQ0BLVDoY4hN9yleFz1870","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport datetime\nts = [('test_1435', 1779950100000),\n      ('wake_up', 1779921000000),\n      ('breakfast', 1779924600000),\n      ('lunch', 1779939000000),\n      ('nap', 1779945000000),\n      ('dinner_next', 1779965100000),\n      ('sleep', 1779897000000),\n      ('run', 1779958800000),\n      ('ALL_runningAt', 1779978197801)]\nfor name, ms in ts:\n    utc = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(ms/1000, tz=datetime.timezone.utc)\n    cst = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(ms/1000, tz=datetime.timezone(datetime.timedelta(hours=8)))\n    print(f'{name:20s} UTC={utc.strftime(\\\"%m-%d %H:%M\\\")}  CST={cst.strftime(\\\"%m-%d %H:%M\\\")}')\n\"\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":1423,"output":4289,"cacheRead":23296,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":29008,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779978311518}}
